## The gluten free diet

You need to cut out **all foods containing gluten**, such as ordinary bread, pasta, cereals, cakes, biscuits, and many sauces and ready meals.

- many foods are naturally gluten free, such as meat, fish, eggs, fruit, vegetables, rice, potatoes and dairy
- look for **gluten free** labels. Allergens, including cereals containing gluten, are shown in **bold** on ingredients lists
- watch out for **cross-contamination**, for example from toasters and shared butter

## Dietitian support

You should be referred to a **dietitian**, who will help you plan a healthy, balanced gluten free diet.

## Oats

Most people with coeliac disease can eat **pure, uncontaminated oats** labelled gluten free. A small number react to them. Check with your dietitian.

## Gluten free food on prescription

Some areas offer gluten free **bread and flour mixes** on prescription. What is available depends on where you live.

## Other treatment

- **vitamin and mineral supplements**, such as iron, calcium or vitamin D, if you are low
- a **bone density scan**, as coeliac disease can cause osteoporosis
- the **pneumococcal vaccine**, as some people with coeliac disease are at higher risk of infections

You should have a review **once a year**.
